{"name":"Star Bright","hex":"#e8ddae","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"star","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any small, natural and bright dot in the sky, most visible in the night or twilight sky. This sense includes the planets, but it is now sometimes used in exclusion of them due to influence from the technical astronomical sense.","A planet thought to influence one's fate; (figuratively) fate or luck."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appear as a featured performer or headliner, especially in an entertainment program.","To feature (a performer or a headliner), especially in a movie or an entertainment program."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/star"},{"term":"bright","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emitting much light; visually dazzling; luminous, lucent, radiant.","Of light: brilliant, intense."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brightness, glow.\n\n Glory, splendour.","Brightness, glow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bright"}],"entries":[{"title":"Star Bright (Vanessa Williams album)","blocks":[{"text":"Star Bright is the fourth studio album and first Christmas album by Vanessa Williams, released on November 5, 1996, on Mercury Records. It achieved success as one of the best-selling holiday albums of 1996 and '97. It peaked at #36 on the Billboard 200, #24 on the Top R&B/Hip-Hop Albums and #5 on the Top Holiday Albums. The album went on to be nominated for a 1998 Grammy Award for Best Pop/Contemporary Gospel Album and was certified gold by the RIAA for sales of over 500,000 copies."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/4/4a/Vanessa_Williams_-_Star_Bright_album_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Bright_(Vanessa_Williams_album)","wikidata":"Q7600648"},{"title":"Star Bright (Dizzy Reece album)","blocks":[{"text":"Star Bright is an album by Jamaican jazz trumpeter Dizzy Reece recorded on November 19, 1959, shortly after his move to New York City, and released on Blue Note the following year."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/78/Star_Bright_%28Dizzy_Reece_album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star_Bright_(Dizzy_Reece_album)","wikidata":"Q7600646"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star","wikidata":"Q523"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Starsinthesky.jpg/500px-Starsinthesky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Starsinthesky.jpg/1280px-Starsinthesky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Starsinthesky.jpg/500px-Starsinthesky.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStarsinthesky.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb